{"ts": 1789777730.7470489, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Confirmed stable and order-invariant. I'll settle on **{38, 13, 3}, all long, IS Sharpe 1.106**.\n\nNow the important part \u2014 forecasting OOS honestly rather than just quoting the in-sample number.\n\n**Sources of expected shrinkage:**\n\n1. **Selection bias.** I evaluated ~65 specs and picked the max. Even with genuine signal, the winner's IS Sharpe is biased upward. The features were chosen *because* they looked best, so some of 1.106 is luck-in-sample.\n2. **Magnitude of that bias.** With n=5000 daily obs, the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly \u221a(1/T) in annualized units \u2248 1/\u221a20 \u2248 0.22... more precisely SE \u2248 \u221a((1+S\u00b2/2)/n) per-period, annualized \u2248 0.16\u00b7\u221a(252/252)... working it through, SE \u2248 0.16. Taking a max over ~65 correlated tests inflates by perhaps 0.5\u20131.0 SE, suggesting ~0.10\u20130.15 of optimistic bias.\n3. **The bar is search-adjusted and I only clear it by 0.09.** That margin is *within* the noise band. The bar rising as I searched is precisely the mechanism warning me that a thin margin is not real edge.\n\n**Countervailing evidence for genuine signal:** the sign-coherence test, the fact that subsets without the top feature still perform well, and the smooth diversification pattern all argue that a substantial core of this is real. This isn't pure noise-mining.\n\nMy point estimate: shrink 1.106 by roughly 0.15\u20130.20 for selection effects, landing near **0.90**. Uncertainty should be generous \u2014 genuine parameter instability, possible regime change, and the correlated-selection effect all widen it; I'll use **sd 0.28**."}
